TR=lambda lab,cap,extra='': S(lab,'',None,cap,B,TREE_INV(110,90,1.6)+extra)
V=[
X(1,'THE UPSIDE-DOWN TREE',
 TR('A great tree with roots above in the sky and branches below','ŪRDHVA-MŪLAM'),
 'The Lord said: They speak of the imperishable aśvattha tree, with roots above and branches below, whose leaves are the Vedic hymns. One who knows it knows the Veda.',
 [('ūrdhva-mūlam adhaḥ-śākham','<b>roots above, branches below</b>'),('aśvattham prāhuḥ avyayam','<b>they call the aśvattha imperishable</b>'),('chandāṃsi yasya parṇāni','<b>whose leaves are the hymns</b>'),('yaḥ tam veda saḥ veda-vit','<b>who knows it knows the Veda</b>')],
 'The chapter opens with a striking image: the world as an upside-down tree.',
 'The image comes from the Kaṭha Upaniṣad (2.3.1). Roots above: the world springs from the Supreme. Branches below: it spreads into our many experiences. The leaves, Vedic hymns, protect and nourish it as leaves protect a tree. <i>Aśvattha</i> may play on <i>a-śvas-tha</i>, “not lasting till tomorrow”: ever-changing, yet endless as a cycle.',
 'Our lives branch out in many directions. Remember where the roots are.',
 'Roots above, <b>branches below</b>.'),
X(2,'BRANCHES AND BUDS',
 TR('Branches spreading up and down, buds of sense objects, roots going down into action','GUṆA-PRAVṚDDHĀḤ'),
 '“Its branches spread below and above, nourished by the guṇas, with sense-objects as buds; and its roots stretch downward too, binding to action in the human world.”',
 [('adhaḥ ca ūrdhvam prasṛtāḥ tasya śākhāḥ','<b>its branches spread below and above</b>'),('guṇa-pravṛddhāḥ viṣaya-pravālāḥ','<b>fed by the guṇas, objects as buds</b>'),('adhaḥ ca mūlāni anusantatāni','<b>and roots stretch downward</b>'),('karma-anubandhīni manuṣya-loke','<b>binding to action in the human world</b>')],
 'The tree grows by the guṇas; its buds are sense objects; secondary roots bind us to action.',
 'Like a banyan, the tree sends roots down from its branches, holding it ever more firmly. Commentators read these as desires and habits created by action in the human world, which keep the tree growing.',
 'Each habit puts down a new root. Notice which roots you are feeding.',
 'Fed by the guṇas, <b>rooted in desire</b>.'),
X(3,'CUT IT WITH DETACHMENT',
 TR('Arjuna swinging an axe of detachment at the tree','ASAṄGA-ŚASTRA',AR(40,120,.7)+'<path d="M60 90 l30 -20" stroke="#ccc" stroke-width="6"/>'),
 '“Its true form is not perceived here, nor its end, nor its beginning, nor its foundation. Having cut this firmly rooted aśvattha with the strong axe of detachment,”',
 [('na rūpam asya iha tathā upalabhyate','<b>its form is not perceived here</b>'),('na antaḥ na ca ādiḥ na ca sampratiṣṭhā','<b>nor end, beginning, nor foundation</b>'),('aśvattham enam su-virūḍha-mūlam','<b>this firmly rooted aśvattha</b>'),('asaṅga-śastreṇa dṛḍhena chittvā','<b>cutting with the strong axe of detachment</b>')],
 'How to be free of the tree: cut it with detachment.',
 'From inside, we can’t see the tree’s shape, beginning or end. But we can cut it. Commentators: the axe is <i>asaṅga</i>, non-attachment, sharpened by discrimination.',
 'You don’t need to understand everything to begin letting go.',
 'Cut it with <b>the axe of detachment</b>.'),
X(4,'THEN SEEK THAT GOAL',
 S('A seeker walking past the fallen tree toward a primal light','Y','light','PRAPADYE'),
 '“…then that goal must be sought, reaching which none return: ‘I take refuge in that primal Person from whom the ancient stream of activity has flowed.’”',
 [('tataḥ padam tat parimārgitavyam','<b>then that state must be sought</b>'),('yasmin gatāḥ na nivartanti bhūyaḥ','<b>going where, none return</b>'),('tam eva ca ādyam puruṣam prapadye','<b>I take refuge in that primal Person</b>'),('yataḥ pravṛttiḥ prasṛtā purāṇī','<b>from whom the ancient stream flowed</b>')],
 'After cutting, seek the source, and take refuge.',
 'Note the prayer inside the verse: <i>tam eva cādyaṃ puruṣaṃ prapadye</i>, “I take refuge in that primal Person”. Commentators note detachment alone is not enough; one must turn toward the source.',
 'Letting go is half; turning toward the good is the other half.',
 'I take refuge in <b>the primal Person</b>.'),
X(5,'WHO REACHES IT',
 S('A humble, free seeker walking to an imperishable abode','Y','light','AMŪḌHĀḤ'),
 '“Free from pride and delusion, having conquered the fault of attachment, ever dwelling in the Self, desires turned away, freed from the pairs called pleasure and pain, the undeluded reach that imperishable goal.”',
 [('nirmāna-mohāḥ jita-saṅga-doṣāḥ','<b>free of pride and delusion, attachment conquered</b>'),('adhyātma-nityāḥ vinivṛtta-kāmāḥ','<b>ever in the Self, desires gone</b>'),('dvandvaiḥ vimuktāḥ sukha-duḥkha-saṃjñaiḥ','<b>freed from opposites called pleasure and pain</b>'),('gacchanti amūḍhāḥ padam avyayam tat','<b>the undeluded reach that imperishable state</b>')],
 'The qualities of those who reach the goal.',
 'Pride (<i>māna</i>) comes first in the list. Commentators: humility is the doorway, again and again in the Gītā (13.7).',
 'Humility and freedom from craving: simple marks of real progress.',
 'Humble and free, <b>they reach it</b>.'),
X(6,'WHERE NO SUN SHINES',
 S('A self-luminous abode where sun, moon and fire are not needed','','light','PARAMAM DHĀMA',NIGHT2,SUN(30,30,10)+MOON(190,30,9)),
 '“Neither the sun, nor the moon, nor fire illumines it. Going there, they do not return. That is my supreme abode.”',
 [('na tat bhāsayate sūryaḥ','<b>the sun does not illumine it</b>'),('na śaśāṅkaḥ na pāvakaḥ','<b>nor the moon, nor fire</b>'),('yat gatvā na nivartante','<b>going where, they do not return</b>'),('tat dhāma paramam mama','<b>that is my supreme abode</b>')],
 'The supreme abode is self-luminous.',
 'This echoes the Kaṭha and Muṇḍaka Upaniṣads: “There the sun does not shine, nor moon nor stars… by its light all this shines.” Commentators: the source of light needs no light.',
 'Some light needs no lamp: awareness itself.',
 'Where <b>no sun is needed</b>.'),
X(7,'A FRAGMENT OF ME',
 S('A spark from Krishna becoming a living being with mind and senses','K','star','MAMA EVA AṂŚAḤ'),
 '“An eternal fragment of myself, having become a living soul (jīva) in the world of the living, draws to itself the mind and the five senses, which rest in prakṛti.”',
 [('mama eva aṃśaḥ jīva-loke','<b>a fragment of me in the world of the living</b>'),('jīva-bhūtaḥ sanātanaḥ','<b>eternal, having become a jīva</b>'),('manaḥ ṣaṣṭhāni indriyāṇi','<b>the senses with mind as sixth</b>'),('prakṛti-sthāni karṣati','<b>resting in nature, it draws</b>')],
 'Each soul is an eternal fragment of God.',
 'A key verse in Vedānta debates. Rāmānuja and Madhva: each soul is eternally a real part (aṃśa) of God, distinct yet dependent. Śaṅkara: the “fragment” is like space in a pot, apparently separate from all-space but really one. All agree the soul is eternal and divine in origin.',
 'You are a spark of the Divine. Remember your dignity, and others’.',
 'A <b>fragment of me</b>.'),
X(8,'LIKE WIND CARRYING FRAGRANCE',
 S('Wind carrying fragrance from flowers to a new place','','storm','LIKE WIND'),
 '“When the lord (the soul) takes on a body and when he leaves it, he takes these (mind and senses) with him, as the wind carries scents from their places.”',
 [('śarīram yat avāpnoti','<b>the body he obtains</b>'),('yat ca api utkrāmati īśvaraḥ','<b>and when the lord departs</b>'),('gṛhītvā etāni saṃyāti','<b>taking these, he goes</b>'),('vāyuḥ gandhān iva āśayāt','<b>as wind carries scents from their source</b>')],
 'At death, the soul carries the subtle mind and senses to the next body.',
 'A lovely image: the wind moves on but carries the fragrance of the garden. Commentators call this the subtle body (sūkṣma śarīra), carried from life to life.',
 'What we cultivate inside travels with us.',
 'Like wind <b>carrying fragrance</b>.'),
X(9,'ENJOYING THROUGH THE SENSES',
 S('The soul presiding over ear, eye, touch, taste, smell and mind','','mind','6 SENSES'),
 '“Presiding over the ear, the eye, touch, taste and smell, and the mind, he enjoys the objects of the senses.”',
 [('śrotram cakṣuḥ sparśanam ca','<b>ear, eye, touch</b>'),('rasanam ghrāṇam eva ca','<b>taste and smell</b>'),('adhiṣṭhāya manaḥ ca ayam','<b>presiding, and over the mind</b>'),('viṣayān upasevate','<b>he enjoys the objects</b>')],
 'The soul experiences the world through mind and senses.',
 'Commentators: the senses are instruments; the soul is the experiencer. This is the same picture as 13.20–21.',
 'Your senses are instruments. You are the one who experiences.',
 'Through the senses, <b>the soul experiences</b>.'),
X(10,'THE DELUDED DO NOT SEE',
 S('People not seeing the soul leaving, staying, enjoying, a sage seeing clearly','Y','light','JÑĀNA-CAKṢUṢAḤ'),
 '“The deluded do not see him departing, staying, or enjoying, joined with the guṇas; those with the eye of knowledge see.”',
 [('utkrāmantam sthitam vā api','<b>departing or staying</b>'),('bhuñjānam vā guṇa-anvitam','<b>or enjoying, with the guṇas</b>'),('vimūḍhāḥ na anupaśyanti','<b>the deluded do not see</b>'),('paśyanti jñāna-cakṣuṣaḥ','<b>those with the eye of knowledge see</b>')],
 'The soul’s journey is unseen except by the wise.',
 'The “eye of knowledge” (compare 13.34) sees what the physical eye misses. Commentators: the soul is present in every experience, yet overlooked.',
 'Look beneath the surface of experience. Someone is experiencing.',
 'Seen only <b>by the eye of knowledge</b>.'),
X(11,'THE STRIVING YOGIS SEE',
 S('Yogis seeing the Self within, others striving in vain','Y','light','YATANTAḤ'),
 '“Striving yogis see him dwelling in themselves; but the unrefined and thoughtless, even when striving, do not see him.”',
 [('yatantaḥ yoginaḥ ca enam','<b>striving yogis, him</b>'),('paśyanti ātmani avasthitam','<b>see dwelling in the self</b>'),('yatantaḥ api akṛta-ātmānaḥ','<b>but even striving, the unrefined</b>'),('na enam paśyanti acetasaḥ','<b>the thoughtless do not see him</b>')],
 'Effort alone is not enough; inner purity is needed.',
 '<i>Akṛtātmā</i>, unrefined, whose mind has not been prepared. Commentators: effort must go with purification, humility and right understanding.',
 'Prepare the ground. Effort on an unready mind bears little fruit.',
 'Effort <b>with purity</b> sees.'),
X(12,'THE LIGHT IN THE SUN',
 S('Sun, moon and fire all shining with Krishna’s light','','sun','MĀMAKAM TEJAḤ'),
 '“The light in the sun that lights the whole world, the light in the moon and in fire: know that light to be mine.”',
 [('yat āditya-gatam tejaḥ','<b>the light in the sun</b>'),('jagat bhāsayate akhilam','<b>which lights the whole world</b>'),('yat candramasi yat ca agnau','<b>which is in the moon and in fire</b>'),('tat tejaḥ viddhi māmakam','<b>know that light as mine</b>')],
 'All light is God’s light.',
 'Verses 15.12–15 show God sustaining the world. Commentators link this to 7.8 and 10.21.',
 'Every light you see is a reminder.',
 'All light <b>is mine</b>.'),
X(13,'NOURISHING THE PLANTS',
 S('Earth supported by Krishna, moonlight nourishing plants','','seed','SOMA'),
 '“Entering the earth, I support all beings with my energy; and becoming the juicy Soma (moon), I nourish all plants.”',
 [('gām āviśya ca bhūtāni','<b>entering the earth, beings</b>'),('dhārayāmi aham ojasā','<b>I support with my energy</b>'),('puṣṇāmi ca auṣadhīḥ sarvāḥ','<b>and I nourish all plants</b>'),('somaḥ bhūtvā rasa-ātmakaḥ','<b>becoming Soma, full of sap</b>')],
 'God holds up the earth and nourishes plants.',
 'In Indian tradition the moon (Soma) nourishes plants with its sap. Commentators: God is both gravity holding us and life in each leaf.',
 'Look at a plant and remember who nourishes it.',
 'I <b>nourish all plants</b>.'),
X(14,'THE FIRE OF DIGESTION',
 S('A fire within the body digesting four kinds of food','P','fire','VAIŚVĀNARA'),
 '“Becoming Vaiśvānara, the fire of life in the bodies of living beings, joined with the in-breath and out-breath, I digest the four kinds of food.”',
 [('aham vaiśvānaraḥ bhūtvā','<b>I, becoming Vaiśvānara</b>'),('prāṇinām deham āśritaḥ','<b>dwelling in the bodies of the living</b>'),('prāṇa-apāna-samāyuktaḥ','<b>joined with prāṇa and apāna</b>'),('pacāmi annam catur-vidham','<b>I digest the four kinds of food</b>')],
 'God is the digestive fire in every body.',
 'The four kinds of food: chewed, sucked, licked and swallowed. This verse is often chanted before meals, and many families recite all of chapter 15 at mealtime. Commentators: eating becomes worship.',
 'Before eating, remember the fire within. Eat gratefully.',
 'I am <b>the fire of digestion</b>.'),
X(15,'SEATED IN EVERY HEART',
 S('Krishna in every heart, the source of memory and knowledge','K','heart','HṚDI'),
 '“I am seated in the hearts of all. From me come memory, knowledge and their loss. I am what is to be known by all the Vedas; I am the author of Vedānta and the knower of the Vedas.”',
 [('sarvasya ca aham hṛdi sanniviṣṭaḥ','<b>I am seated in the heart of all</b>'),('mattaḥ smṛtiḥ jñānam apohanam ca','<b>from me memory, knowledge, and their loss</b>'),('vedaiḥ ca sarvaiḥ aham eva vedyaḥ','<b>I am to be known by all the Vedas</b>'),('vedānta-kṛt veda-vit eva ca aham','<b>maker of Vedānta, knower of the Vedas</b>')],
 'God within the heart, the source of memory and understanding.',
 'A rich verse. Even forgetting comes from him, which commentators read as a mercy (we forget sorrows) or as the working of karma. “All the Vedas point to me”: the goal of scripture is God.',
 'The one you seek is already seated in your heart.',
 '<b>Seated in every heart</b>.'),
X(16,'THE PERISHABLE AND IMPERISHABLE',
 S('Two figures: changing beings and the unchanging','','scale','KṢARA · AKṢARA'),
 '“There are two persons in this world: the perishable and the imperishable. All beings are the perishable; the unchanging one is called the imperishable.”',
 [('dvau imau puruṣau loke','<b>two persons in the world</b>'),('kṣaraḥ ca akṣaraḥ eva ca','<b>the perishable and imperishable</b>'),('kṣaraḥ sarvāṇi bhūtāni','<b>the perishable is all beings</b>'),('kūṭa-sthaḥ akṣaraḥ ucyate','<b>the unchanging is called imperishable</b>')],
 'Two kinds of “person”: what changes, and what does not.',
 'Commentators differ: for Śaṅkara, kṣara is the world of change and akṣara is māyā, its unchanging seed; for Rāmānuja, kṣara is bound souls in bodies and akṣara is freed souls. Either way, a third is beyond both.',
 'Notice what changes in you and what does not.',
 'Perishable <b>and imperishable</b>.'),
X(17,'THE SUPREME PERSON',
 S('A third Person beyond both, pervading three worlds','','vishnu','PARAMĀTMĀ'),
 '“But there is another, the highest Person, called the Supreme Self, the imperishable Lord, who enters and sustains the three worlds.”',
 [('uttamaḥ puruṣaḥ tu anyaḥ','<b>but another, the highest Person</b>'),('paramātmā iti udāhṛtaḥ','<b>called the Supreme Self</b>'),('yaḥ loka-trayam āviśya','<b>who, entering the three worlds</b>'),('bibharti avyayaḥ īśvaraḥ','<b>sustains, the imperishable Lord</b>')],
 'Beyond both: the Supreme Person.',
 'This verse names the theme of the chapter: Puruṣottama. Commentators note even the imperishable needs the Lord as its support.',
 'There is a support beneath all supports.',
 'Beyond both: <b>the Supreme Person</b>.'),
X(18,'PURUṢOTTAMA',
 S('Krishna named the Supreme Person, above the perishable and imperishable','K','crown','PURUṢOTTAMA'),
 '“Because I am beyond the perishable and higher even than the imperishable, I am celebrated in the world and in the Veda as Puruṣottama, the Supreme Person.”',
 [('yasmāt kṣaram atītaḥ aham','<b>since I am beyond the perishable</b>'),('akṣarāt api ca uttamaḥ','<b>and higher than the imperishable</b>'),('ataḥ asmi loke vede ca','<b>so I am, in the world and the Veda</b>'),('prathitaḥ puruṣottamaḥ','<b>celebrated as Puruṣottama</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa names himself Puruṣottama.',
 'The chapter is named Puruṣottama Yoga. Vaiṣṇava traditions take this as a key verse for the supremacy of the personal Lord. Śaṅkara reads Puruṣottama as Brahman beyond the world and its seed.',
 'The highest is not only a principle but a Person who can be loved.',
 'I am <b>Puruṣottama</b>.'),
X(19,'WITH THE WHOLE BEING',
 S('A devotee worshipping with all his heart','KA','heart','SARVA-BHĀVENA'),
 '“One who, undeluded, knows me thus as the Supreme Person knows everything, Bhārata, and worships me with his whole being.”',
 [('yaḥ mām evam asammūḍhaḥ','<b>who, undeluded, thus me</b>'),('jānāti puruṣottamam','<b>knows as the Supreme Person</b>'),('saḥ sarva-vit bhajati mām','<b>he, all-knowing, worships me</b>'),('sarva-bhāvena bhārata','<b>with his whole being</b>')],
 'Knowing God fully leads to worship with one’s whole being.',
 'Knowledge and devotion become one: “knows everything” and “worships with all his heart”. Commentators see this as the meeting point of jñāna and bhakti.',
 'True knowing ends in love.',
 'Knowing, he loves <b>with his whole being</b>.'),
X(20,'THE MOST SECRET TEACHING',
 S('Krishna completing a secret teaching, Arjuna enlightened','KA','book','GUHYATAMAM'),
 '“Thus this most secret teaching has been told by me, sinless one. Understanding this, one becomes wise and fulfils all one’s duties, Bhārata.”',
 [('iti guhyatamam śāstram','<b>thus the most secret teaching</b>'),('idam uktam mayā anagha','<b>this has been told by me, sinless one</b>'),('etat buddhvā buddhimān syāt','<b>knowing this, one becomes wise</b>'),('kṛta-kṛtyaḥ ca bhārata','<b>and has done all that is to be done</b>')],
 'The chapter ends: this is the most secret teaching.',
 '<i>Kṛta-kṛtya</i>, “one who has done what is to be done”: life’s purpose fulfilled. Commentators say chapter 15 is a summary of the whole Vedānta in 20 verses.',
 'A short chapter can hold a whole philosophy. Learn it by heart.',
 'Knowing this, <b>life is fulfilled</b>.'),
]
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="A great upside-down ashvattha tree with roots in a glowing sky and branches spreading below, a small figure with an axe of detachment">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#0b2a4a" rx="8"/>'+GLOW(190,40,80,'#ffd60a',.35)+TREE_INV(190,130,3.4)+AR(60,236,.8)+'<path d="M80 200 l40 -30" stroke="#ccc" stroke-width="8"/>'+'</svg>')
P=dict(ch=15,nv=20,V=V,per=[20],out='/home/claude/work/gita/Gita_Chapter15.html',
 title='The Supreme Person · Bhagavad Gītā Chapter 15 · srini.presents',
 desc='Bhagavad Gītā chapter 15, Puruṣottama Yoga, every śloka explained: the upside-down aśvattha tree, the axe of detachment, the self-luminous abode, the soul as an eternal fragment of the Lord, the fire of digestion, and the Supreme Person beyond the perishable and imperishable. All 20 ślokas in IAST, each on two pages.',
 burst='CHAPTER 15 OF 18',h1='THE SUPREME PERSON',series='BHAGAVAD GĪTĀ · CHAPTER 15 · PURUṢOTTAMA YOGA',tag='The upside-down tree · every śloka explained',cover=cover,
 where=[f'In {chl(14)} Kṛṣṇa explained the three guṇas and how devotion carries one beyond them.',
  'Chapter 15 is short and deep, and many families recite it before meals. It pictures the world as an upside-down tree with roots in the Supreme, to be cut with detachment. Each soul is an eternal fragment of the Lord; he lights the sun, nourishes plants and digests our food; and he is Puruṣottama, beyond the perishable and the imperishable.'],
 wtalk=("Why recite it before meals?","15.14 says the Lord is the fire that digests our food. The whole chapter turns a meal into a prayer."),
 fullnote=FULLNOTE,
 secs=[('THE ASVATTHA TREE',1),('THE SUPREME ABODE',5),('THE SOUL',7),('THE LORD IN ALL',12),('PURUṢOTTAMA',16)],
 end=['<b>The tree (15.1–4).</b> The world is an upside-down aśvattha, roots above; cut it with detachment and seek the primal Person.',
  '<b>The abode (15.5–6).</b> Reached by the humble and free; lit by no sun, moon or fire.',
  '<b>The soul (15.7–11).</b> An eternal fragment of the Lord, carrying mind and senses from body to body like wind carries scent.',
  '<b>The Lord in all (15.12–15).</b> The light in the sun, the sap in plants, the fire of digestion, seated in every heart.',
  '<b>Puruṣottama (15.16–20).</b> Beyond the perishable and the imperishable; knowing him, one worships with the whole being.'],
 endsub='Chapter 15 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",f'{chl(16)}: Divine and demonic. Twenty-six divine qualities, and the opposite nature: pride, anger, greed. And the three gates of hell.'),
 endkey='<b>cut the tree of craving; take refuge in the Supreme Person</b>.',
 foot=FOOT(15,'Parallels with the Kaṭha and Muṇḍaka Upaniṣads are noted by traditional commentators. '))
build2(P)
